  sectioned design, then a written spec. Say the word if you'd rather I just do the minimal extraction inline.

  First question — this is the one that decides what "easier to change environments" actually means.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether switching environments is an [3medit[0m (someone changes a value in a file before deploying) or a
  [3mdetection[0m (the code figures out where it's running and picks the matching endpoint at load time). That changes the
  settings module's shape: the edit path exports one flat config object; the detection path exports a per-environment
  table plus a resolver, and the resolver's input (hostname? query param? a value stamped in by deploy tooling?) becomes
  an interface you're stuck with.

  - [1mHostname detection[0m — zero ceremony at deploy time, works with the no-build setup, and a [38;5;153m?env=[39m override makes local
    testing against staging easy. Costs you a hostname table that has to be kept accurate, and it's client-visible (all
    endpoints ship to every browser). Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSingle edited value[0m — dead simple, one obvious place to change. Costs a manual step per environment, which is
    exactly the error-prone thing you're presumably trying to kill. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — the most robust and the standard answer at scale, but it means adding a bundler or a
    substitution script to a repo that currently has neither. Expensive to undo, and it drags the Node side in too.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection with a query-param override.[0m It fits a no-build static page, makes the switch
  automatic, and keeps the door open to build-time injection later since the consumer-facing interface
  ([38;5;153mSettings.apiEndpoint[39m) stays the same either way.
